<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Retained paired spot + top-of-book series (issue #170). DOCUMENTED, NOT
  AUTO-LOADED — live infrastructure is bounced deliberately: the operator
  INSTALLS the script, copies this file into ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ and loads
  it by hand:

      python3 openmarketterminal-qt/scripts/kalshi_lag_series.py install
      cp org.openterminal.lag-series.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.openterminal.lag-series.plist

  THE FIRST STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND IT IS ALSO THE STEP TO REPEAT AFTER ANY
  EDIT TO THE SCRIPT (issue #183). This job used to run the script straight out
  of the git working tree. That made the recorder's uptime a side effect of
  whichever commit a developer's tree happened to sit on: on 2026-07-28 a
  checkout of a commit older than the script left every 15-minute invocation
  dying with `[Errno 2] No such file or directory` for ~10 hours — past the
  rotation horizon below, i.e. book history that no later run can recover. A
  rename or a `git clean` had the same power. It now runs an installed copy
  under the app data root, where git has no opinion:

      ~/Library/Application Support/org.openterminal.OpenTerminal/libexec/

  Every 15 minutes it appends a downsampled distillate of kalshi-tickers.jsonl
  and kalshi-cf-benchmarks.jsonl (stdlib only, every evidence log opened
  read-only) to

      ~/Library/Application Support/Open Terminal/Open Terminal/kalshi-lag-series/

  THE INTERVAL IS LOAD-BEARING. kalshi-tickers.jsonl rotates by SIZE, turning
  over in ~5.7 hours at the current write rate, and the rotation it displaces
  is gone. Anything that keeps this job from running for longer than that
  loses book history permanently — which is the whole defect issue #170
  exists to fix. A missed window is not silent: the compactor compares its
  cursor against the oldest row the source still holds and records the
  uncovered span in the manifest's `gaps`.

  Verify after the first run, and whenever you want to know it is still alive:

      python3 openmarketterminal-qt/scripts/kalshi_lag_series.py status
      python3 openmarketterminal-qt/scripts/kalshi_lag_series.py doctor

  `status` prints the retained span, row counts, cadence, any gaps and the age
  of the last successful compaction — shouting, and exiting nonzero, once that
  age passes the rotation horizon. `doctor` reads the plist actually loaded in
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents and says whether the file it names exists and is the
  installed copy, which is the failure launchctl reports only as "status 2".
  If this job never runs, the series directory is simply absent, the research
  scripts read exactly the logs they read before it existed, and nothing is
  fabricated.
-->
<plist version="1.0"><dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>org.openterminal.lag-series</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key><array>
    <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
    <string>/Users/haydarevich/Library/Application Support/org.openterminal.OpenTerminal/libexec/kalshi_lag_series.py</string>
    <string>compact</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>900</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/Users/haydarevich/Library/Application Support/org.openterminal.OpenTerminal/logs/lag-series.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/haydarevich/Library/Application Support/org.openterminal.OpenTerminal/logs/lag-series.err.log</string>
</dict></plist>
